Full passage:
According to the Stoics, some impressions are undeniably veridical—they reflect reality and bear the mark of truth—while others are not. For instance, if I [the observer] am certain that I’m seeing my brother across the street, and it really turns out to be my brother, then I’m having an undeniably veridical impression, which Zeno called kataleptic, from the Greek word for “grasping.” I am, in a sense, firmly grasping the truth. Conversely, if it turns out that it was my brother’s doppelgänger across the street, then my impression was false, akataleptic (without grasping the truth), despite the fact that I was strongly inclined to accept it (after all, how can I be wrong about my own brother?).

We can all have both kataleptic and akataleptic impressions, but only the sage, the perfect human reasoner, is capable of distinguishing for certain between the two types of impressions, thus acquiring knowledge. The sage, unlike the rest of us, can somehow always tell my brother from his doppelgänger, thereby assenting only to true impressions.

Nonsense, responded the skeptics. Even if there were sages out there—a doubtful proposition in its own right—they wouldn’t be able to tell true from false impressions for the simple reason that all impressions are akataleptic. That is, none of them bear any special mark of truth. Here is the argument as presented by Cicero himself:[10]

Premise 1: There are true and false impressions;

Premise 2: False impressions are akataleptic;

Premise 3: True impressions are indistinguishable from false ones that appear identical to them;

Premise 4: If two impressions present us with no perceptible difference, it is not possible for one to be kataleptic and the other akataleptic;

Conclusion: There are no such things as kataleptic impressions.

Consider again my brother and his doppelgänger. The skeptics’ conviction is that nobody—including sages—can ever know for sure whether they are looking at one or the other. Notice that the claim is not that there is no truth in the matter: Cicero would readily agree that in reality there is my brother and then there is his doppelgänger, and they are distinct entities. But it is always possible that I may not be able to tell the difference, even if I were a sage. In other words, skeptics like Cicero are not making a metaphysical statement about what does or does not exist, but rather an epistemological one about what we can and cannot know.

This is important because it shows why a standard Stoic rejoinder missed the mark. Anticipating a problem formulated in the seventeenth century by Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz, the Stoics argued that if two things are in fact distinct—like my brother and his doppelgänger—then they cannot be identical: there must be some difference between them, however small, apparently insignificant, or hard to find.[11] This does not actually help, because it is a metaphysical statement (known in modern philosophy as the identity of indiscernibles), not an epistemological one. The Stoics and the skeptics were talking past each other. It is now time to turn to the two schools of skepticism and see what they had to say about the all-important question: How do you know?
